Michal Brzozowski <rusolis@poczta.fm> wrote:

>I'm creating a separate thread, mostly to cut off from the ongoing
>flamewar in the one started by JG Miller.  I agree that anniversaries
>are a good time for reflection so let's try to evaluate the current
>situation of Nethack and its community and what can be done to make
>things better (as opposed to concentrating on what's wrong with the
>Devteam).


Good effort with this thread - this is an important step.  And I don't
mind being a bad cop here.  But I see some of the same patterns
emerging in the conversation yet again, though this is not your fault.
There's a lot of pontificating and dick stroking in relation to
various writers' command of or feverish opinions on various
programming languages, and very little coming together, no pun
intended.  If the past is any guide, this will go on for a little
while longer and then die out as the conversation du jour moves on to
some strategy question or flaming someone for the formatting of their
posts.  This has been happening for years.  Many of the old-timers
here seem to prefer this rhythm and the status quo arrangement suits
them fine.

The more new blood that comes into these conversations, the better the
chance of something actually happening.

Again, I think criticism of the dev team is not out of line on this
matter.  They've done a lot of good things to bring the game to the
state it is.  That doesn't negate the fact that they could be, but
aren't, helping to facilitate the community's apparent interest in
further developing the game in a revamped way.  They could contribute
to a new effort, for example, by simply releasing the code for all
those "fixed" bugs that have been lying round for 5 years - that would
be a tremendous offering and would require little further effort on
their part beyond what they've already completed.

But there's the received wisdom I've heard here several times - that
there's no chance the dev team is going to cooperate with anyone, and
that's the end of the matter.  Underlying that, I think, is the belief
that the dev team shouldn't feel obligated to cooperate with the
community - everyone for himself, redux.  That belief is unfortunate,
and I think wrong.

